WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A designer draws six panels for a gift box, but the lid will
not close.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.720 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is the problem the number of faces, their dimensions, or
their arrangement?

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will use evidence to repair a design.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.759 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A drawing can look convincing while still hiding a real
construction problem.

00:00:15.759 --> 00:00:16.159 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to design, test and improve a net for
packaging.

00:00:16.159 --> 00:00:27.359 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will move from objects or hands-on actions to drawings,
then connect those drawings with precise mathematical
descriptions.

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:28.719 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep paper nearby if you would like to test an idea for
yourself.

00:00:28.719 --> 00:00:34.118 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can pause the player whenever you need more time.

00:00:34.118 --> 00:00:38.238 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to design, test and improve a net for
packaging.

00:00:38.238 --> 00:00:41.038 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We want to explain why a result is correct, as well as
obtain the result.

00:00:41.038 --> 00:00:48.430 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you watch, look for the evidence that would help you
convince a partner who has not seen your working.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:51.110 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Here are our three success criteria.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:53.390 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can plan the required faces.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.910 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can check dimensions and joins.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:58.230 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can explain a useful repair.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:02.070 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will check these ideas in two worked examples,
investigate a common mistake and finish with an independent
challenge.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:10.803 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A good explanation links what you see in the model with the
mathematical rule being used.

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:14.083 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use scrap paper before cutting expensive card.

00:01:14.083 --> 00:01:18.323 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw fold lines, label each panel and mark the intended
base.

00:01:18.323 --> 00:01:22.003 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Cut only around the outside with adult help when needed.

00:01:22.003 --> 00:01:24.563 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Gently fold along shared edges.

00:01:24.563 --> 00:01:29.597 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Testing a small prototype helps you find errors while
changes are still easy.

00:01:29.597 --> 00:01:34.757 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine a rectangular box two units long, one unit wide and
one unit high.

00:01:34.757 --> 00:01:40.157 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It needs two long horizontal faces, two long side faces, and
two square end faces.

00:01:40.157 --> 00:01:41.877 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Opposite faces match.

00:01:41.877 --> 00:01:46.845 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Listing the required faces gives us a useful check before we
choose an arrangement.

00:01:46.845 --> 00:01:53.205 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our box needs four rectangles measuring two units by one
unit and two squares measuring one by one.

00:01:53.205 --> 00:01:57.085 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A shared fold edge must have the same length on both panels.

00:01:57.085 --> 00:02:02.934 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Equal numbers of panels do not help if one intended join is
longer on one side than the other.

00:02:02.934 --> 00:02:03.454 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the band of four rectangles.

00:02:03.454 --> 00:02:10.854 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The two square end faces must close opposite openings when
the band wraps around.

00:02:10.854 --> 00:02:13.654 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Should both squares try to cover the same end?

00:02:13.654 --> 00:02:18.825 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict where to place them, then explain which opening each
square is meant to close.

00:02:23.825 --> 00:02:27.985 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The four rectangles wrap into the four long faces of the
box.

00:02:27.985 --> 00:02:29.945 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Attach one square to each side of the band so they close
opposite ends.

00:02:29.945 --> 00:02:34.785 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The short edges match the square sides.

00:02:34.785 --> 00:02:39.963 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We have accounted for every face and assigned each panel a
different place on the solid.

00:02:39.963 --> 00:02:44.843 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A fold line joins two faces that remain connected in the
flat design.

00:02:44.843 --> 00:02:49.123 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A cut separates edges that will meet later when the package
closes.

00:02:49.123 --> 00:02:52.723 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Marking these differently helps someone else build your
design.

00:02:52.723 --> 00:02:58.217 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A glue tab lies outside a face and supports a join; it does
not change the face count.

00:02:58.217 --> 00:03:05.577 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A two by three rectangle of six squares contains enough
square material for a cube, but it does not fold into one
correctly.

00:03:05.577 --> 00:03:08.097 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Do not repair it by stretching a face.

00:03:08.097 --> 00:03:10.137 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep equal squares and change the connection pattern.

00:03:10.137 --> 00:03:15.420 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The geometry of the intended cube must remain unchanged.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.220 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Place four equal squares in a straight column.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.540 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Attach one square on each side of the second square.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:25.260 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check that every square can be reached across a shared whole
edge.

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:31.791 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This connected cross gives the base four immediate
neighbours and leaves the final square available as a lid.

00:03:31.791 --> 00:03:34.951 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare the invalid rectangle with the cross.

00:03:34.951 --> 00:03:41.551 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can you explain why moving panels changes the result even
though the total number of squares stays six?

00:03:41.551 --> 00:03:44.671 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the role of the last square in the cross.

00:03:44.671 --> 00:03:49.265 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use face positions rather than saying that one drawing just
looks better.

00:03:54.265 --> 00:03:56.705 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Fold the cross mentally or with paper.

00:03:56.705 --> 00:04:00.065 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The base stays still, four neighbouring squares form walls,
and the last square closes the top.

00:04:00.065 --> 00:04:06.745 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The repair works because the six panels occupy six different
face positions.

00:04:06.745 --> 00:04:11.547 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A successful test explains the change; it is more than a
neat final drawing.

00:04:11.547 --> 00:04:16.827 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Label an outside edge before folding and find the edge that
meets it after folding.

00:04:16.827 --> 00:04:18.507 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their lengths must match.

00:04:18.507 --> 00:04:20.347 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Repeat around the opening.

00:04:20.347 --> 00:04:21.267 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This catches a wrongly sized lid even when the overall
arrangement is sensible.

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:29.671 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A gap can come from a missing face or from an incorrect
dimension.

00:04:29.671 --> 00:04:34.631 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A useful design record says what failed, what changed and
why the change should help.

00:04:34.631 --> 00:04:40.551 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For example, two panels competed for the same face position,
so we moved one to cover the empty top.

00:04:40.551 --> 00:04:45.181 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This explanation lets another designer reproduce and
evaluate your repair.

00:04:45.181 --> 00:04:49.741 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Colouring or rotating the whole net does not fix its fold
relationships.

00:04:49.741 --> 00:04:54.581 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Those changes affect the page view, but do not change which
faces share edges.

00:04:54.581 --> 00:05:02.581 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A genuine geometric repair changes a dimension or connection
responsible for the failure while preserving the intended
solid.

00:05:02.581 --> 00:05:04.421 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Your partner says a box net is correct because opposite
faces have matching dimensions.

00:05:04.421 --> 00:05:10.141 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Is that enough evidence?

00:05:10.141 --> 00:05:14.101 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about what else a flat design must do when it folds.

00:05:14.101 --> 00:05:19.492 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Prepare a short checklist a younger designer could use
without already knowing the answer.

00:05:24.492 --> 00:05:29.292 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check the required faces, the lengths of meeting edges, and
the folded positions.

00:05:29.292 --> 00:05:30.532 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
All three matter.

00:05:30.532 --> 00:05:34.612 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A net can have the right shapes and dimensions but still
overlap.

00:05:34.612 --> 00:05:42.099 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A prototype that closes correctly, together with a clear
explanation, gives much stronger evidence than any single
check alone.

00:05:42.099 --> 00:05:43.939 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to our learning goal.

00:05:43.939 --> 00:05:46.979 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Design, test and improve a net for packaging.

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:51.979 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose one example from today and explain the evidence that
made its answer convincing.

00:05:51.979 --> 00:05:56.619 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which of our three success criteria feels secure, and which
needs another try?

00:05:56.619 --> 00:06:03.162 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Revisit a model, test your explanation with a partner, and
describe one mistake you can now notice and correct.
